Skill anomaly_investigate {
  label: "Investigate anomaly"
  invocation: "manual"
  description: '''
    Investigates whether a specific metric value at a specific time point is statistically
    unusual given its history.

    Use when the user has a metric in mind and a suspicious time point, e.g.:
      • "Is revenue on Jan 15 unusual?"
      • "Daily signups dropped on Tuesday — investigate"
      • "/anomaly_investigate" with no args (skill will ask for the metric + date)

    Out of scope: scanning for anomalies across many metrics, ongoing monitoring,
    dimensional attribution (what drove the anomaly), or speculation on causes.
  '''
  content: @md
    # Anomaly Investigation Playbook

    You are running a manually-invoked anomaly investigation. The user has named a metric
    and a suspicious time point, and wants to know: **is this value unusual?**

    Run two stages: **Establish Expectation**, then **Confirm Anomaly**. Do NOT speculate
    on causes or attempt to identify which dimension drove the anomaly — those require
    deeper investigation outside this skill.

    ## Tool rule — never replace the user's canvas; emit only inline artifacts

    The user may already be viewing a chart on the canvas (a dashboard tile, an exploration,
    a saved view). This skill must never overwrite or rerender it. Every artifact this skill
    surfaces — chart, table, anything visible — MUST appear as a fresh inline artifact in
    the chat, alongside the user's existing canvas state, never in its place.

    - **Use:** `execute_viz` — renders a new chat-inline viz. Leaves canvas alone.
    - **Use:** `execute_aql` — returns data to this conversation; treat the result as
      internal state for narrative computation. Do not promote it into a user-facing table.
    - **Never use:** `execute_viz_block` or any other `_block` / canvas-targeting variant
      — these rerender an existing tile in place and will replace whatever the user is
      currently viewing.

    **Intermediate AQL results are internal, not output.** The history series (Establish
    Expectation Step 1) and the classified series (Step 4) are state the skill computes
    over. Do **not** echo them back to the user as a table or a chart. The only artifacts
    the user sees from this skill are the final chart and the final summary prose, both
    defined in the Output Format section.

    This rule holds even when the user's current chart shows the same metric, dataset, or
    time range as the investigation. It holds even when the user explicitly says "update the
    chart", "refresh it", or "just show me the data" — in this skill, those still mean a
    new chat-inline artifact, never a tile rerender or canvas replacement. There is no
    exception.

    ## Tool invocation conventions

    These conventions apply to every Holistics MCP call the skill makes.

    ### Titles for chat-inline artifacts

    `execute_aql` and `execute_viz` both require a `title` parameter — this is the label
    users see on the chat-inline artifact. Use these patterns (keep under ~60 characters):

    | Call site | Title pattern |
    |---|---|
    | Timeframe data-extent probe `execute_aql` | `<metric> data extent` |
    | Step 1 history-series `execute_aql` | `<metric> history (<resolved timeframe>)` |
    | Step 4 classified-series `execute_aql` | `<metric> baseline check (<resolved timeframe>)` |
    | Output-format chart `execute_viz` | `Anomaly check: <metric> on <resolved time_point>` |

    Avoid generic titles like "query 1" or "untitled" — the artifact remains in the user's
    chat history.

    ### Validate before executing

    For each AQL query composed via `generate_aql`, call `validate_aql` immediately before
    `execute_aql`. This catches syntax and semantic errors cheaply — critical for the Step 4
    query, which uses window functions, partitions, and computed columns.

    If `validate_aql` returns errors:

    1. Regenerate by calling `generate_aql` again with refinement context (see below).
    2. Re-validate the regenerated AQL.
    3. If it still fails after one retry, fall back to the AQL-fallback escape hatch
       described in Step 4, or stop with a clear error message naming the failed stage.

    ### Refinement context on regeneration

    When regenerating AQL or a viz after a failure or refinement request, always pass the
    previous attempt as context — the generation tools rely on these fields to learn what
    didn't work:

    - `generate_aql`: pass `current_aql=<previous AQL>` and `current_result=<previous
      result or validation/execution error text, including row limits>`.
    - `generate_viz`: pass `current_viz=<previous viz>` and `current_result=<previous
      result or error text>`.

    ### Handling truncated results

    Holistics MCP may return `@TruncatedContext:<chunk_id>` placeholders inside a large
    result instead of inlining the full data. When this happens, call
    `fetch_context(chunk_id=<id>)` for each placeholder to retrieve the full chunk before
    reasoning over the data. Daily metrics over multi-year timeframes are the typical
    trigger.

    ## Inputs to extract

    Parse the user's invocation message for these four fields:

    | Field | Required | Examples | If missing |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | `metric` | **Yes** | `revenue`, `signups`, `MRR`, `daily_active_users` | **Ask the user. Do not guess.** |
    | `time_point` | **Yes** | `Jan 15`, `last Tuesday`, `2026-01-15`, `week of Feb 3` | **Ask the user. Do not guess.** |
    | `granularity` | Inferred | `day`, `week`, `month`, `quarter` | Derive from `time_point` phrasing combined with the dataset's time dimension. If still ambiguous, ask. |
    | `filters` | No | `Region=West`, `Plan=Pro`, `Country=US` | Optional. If present, propagate to **every** AQL call. |
    | `timeframe` | No | `last 90 days`, `last 6 months`, `since Jan 2025`, `last 1 year` | Optional. Default by granularity: `day` → 90 days, `week` → 26 weeks, `month` → 24 months, `quarter` → 12 quarters. The timeframe is the **historical window** the skill compares `time_point` against — ends one bucket before `time_point`. |

    Echo the extracted values back to the user before proceeding so they can correct any mis-parses. Always show the **resolved timeframe** as an absolute date range (e.g., *"Timeframe: Aug 19, 2025 – Jan 14, 2026"*), even when the user didn't specify one — the default was applied.

    ## Dataset resolution

    Anchor the investigation to **one** dataset per invocation. Resolve it as follows:

    1. **If the user named a dataset** (e.g., `…in the sales dataset…`) → call `fetch_dataset(dataset_uname=<the user's name>)` and proceed.
    2. **Else:**
       a. Call `list_datasets()`.
       b. Rank candidates by how well the user's metric name matches each dataset's measures,
          labels, and descriptions. Prefer matches in measures over matches in dataset names.
       c. Call `fetch_dataset(dataset_uname=<top candidate>)` to inspect its measures.
       d. **Confirm with the user before proceeding**, e.g.:
          *"I'll use the `sales_orders` dataset (has measure `total_revenue`). Confirm,
          or name a different dataset."*
       e. Wait for the user's confirmation. Do NOT continue investigation until they confirm.
    3. **Fallback** — if `list_datasets()` returns an error (e.g., "not supported in
       development mode"), ask the user to name the dataset explicitly.

    ### Validate the metric

    Once a dataset is resolved, confirm the named `metric` exists as a measure in the dataset's
    measures list. If not:
    - Show the top-3 closest measure names by string similarity, with their descriptions.
    - Ask the user which to use, or to rename the metric.

    ### Bind the time axis

    Find the dataset's date/timestamp dimension. If multiple exist, prefer in order:
    a. the dimension already tagged as primary in the dataset metadata
    b. the one most commonly referenced by the metric's measure definition
    c. a dimension named `event_date`, `created_at`, `date`, or `timestamp` (in that order)

    If none of these rules picks a unique dimension, ask the user which to use.

    Resolve relative time references (e.g., `last Tuesday`) in the dataset's stated timezone.
    Echo the resolved absolute date back to the user before proceeding.

    ### Resolve the timeframe

    Resolve the `timeframe` field into an absolute date range:

    - If the user supplied a timeframe, parse it (relative phrases like *"last 6 months"*
      and *"since Jan 2025"* allowed) in the dataset's stated timezone, anchored to
      `time_point`. The range ends **one bucket before** `time_point`.
    - If the user did NOT supply a timeframe, apply the granularity default from the
      Inputs table.

    Validate the resolved range against the dataset's data extent — optionally via a small
    `min(bucket), max(bucket)` probe AQL on the bound time axis with the user's filters
    applied. If the requested timeframe extends earlier than `min(bucket)`, clamp to
    `min(bucket)` and tell the user the actual range used. If the resulting range yields
    fewer than 10 buckets, treat as the `n_points < 10` degrade case and stop.

    Echo the resolved timeframe back to the user as an absolute date range alongside the
    other extracted inputs, so the user can correct any mis-parses or extend the timeframe
    before the skill commits to AQL work.

    ## Establish Expectation

    Goal: produce an `expected_value` and `spread` (σ-equivalent) for the user's
    `time_point`, plus the confidence range.

    ### Step 1 — Probe history

    Call `generate_aql` to construct a query that returns the metric, grouped by the bound
    time axis, at the user's granularity, with the user's filters applied, over the
    **resolved timeframe** (from the dataset-resolution stage). The timeframe deliberately
    ends one bucket before `time_point` so the suspect value never contaminates the
    baseline — `time_point` itself is added to the classified series in Step 4, not here.

    Then `validate_aql` the generated query (see Tool invocation conventions). Once
    validation passes, call `execute_aql` with `title="<metric> history (<resolved
    timeframe>)"` to retrieve the series. Call this the **history series**.

    The history series is **internal state**, not user-facing output. Do not render it as
    a table, chart, or any other artifact to the user. Do not include rows or summaries of
    it in the conversation. It exists only to feed the Step 2 inspection and the Step 4
    AQL query. The user's first visible artifact from this skill is the final chart in the
    Output Format section.

    ### Step 2 — Inspect the series

    Compute three properties:

    - `n_points` — the count of non-null values in the history series
    - `weekday_pattern_score` (daily granularity only) —
      `stdev(mean_per_weekday) / mean(stdev_within_weekday)`
      Ratio of between-weekday variability to within-weekday variability. A value `> 1.5`
      indicates the day-of-week effect dominates random noise.
    - `noise_ratio` = `MAD(series) / median(series)` — relative variability

    Do these arithmetically in narrative reasoning over the series returned by `execute_aql`.
    No second AQL call needed.

    **Degrade check:** if `n_points < 10`, stop now and report *"Not enough history (n=X).
    Need ≥10 comparable points to compute a baseline."* End the investigation.

    ### Step 3 — Offer the user a baseline method

    Present the **eligible** baseline methods to the user with a short rationale per method,
    and let them choose. Eligibility depends on the data characteristics from Step 2 — only
    list methods that are eligible.

    **Naming.** Every method has a friendly name (what the user sees) and a short internal id
    (what the skill stores in the classified-series record and uses in the AQL formula table):

    | User-facing name | Internal id | What it does |
    |---|---|---|
    | **All time** | `mad` | Compares to the typical historical value. Best for stable metrics or short history. |
    | **Weekly basis** | `sdow` | Compares this `<weekday name>` to recent `<weekday name>s`. Best when there's a clear day-of-week pattern. |
    | **Yearly basis** | `yoy` | Compares to the same period last year, adjusted for recent growth. Best for monthly metrics with annual patterns. |
    | **Rolling basis** | `rolling` | Averages the most recent comparable periods. Best for metrics that drift gradually without a strong cycle. |

    Always use the **user-facing name** in messages to the user (prompts, announcements,
    summary prose). Use the **internal id** in the classified-series state record and in
    AQL-formula bindings.

    Build a prompt like this:

    > "Over **<resolved timeframe as date range>**, I have **<n_points>** data points for
    > `<metric>` at **<granularity>** granularity.
    > Inspection: <plain-language summary, see translation rules below>.
    >
    > How should I set the expected range?
    >
    > 1. **Let me pick** (recommended) — I'll choose based on the data.
    > 2. **All time** — compares to the typical historical value. Best for stable metrics or short history.
    > 3. **Weekly basis** — compares this <weekday name> to recent <weekday name>s. Best when there's a clear day-of-week pattern.
    > 4. **Yearly basis** — compares to the same period last year, adjusted for recent growth. Best for monthly metrics with annual patterns.
    > 5. **Rolling basis** — averages the most recent comparable periods. Best for steadily trending metrics with no strong cycle.
    >
    > Reply with the number or the name."

    **Plain-language inspection summary.** When filling the `Inspection:` line, translate
    the numeric properties from Step 2 into everyday wording — never surface the raw
    `weekday_pattern_score` or `noise_ratio` numbers to the user.

    - `weekday_pattern_score` (daily granularity only): `> 1.5` → "strong weekly pattern";
      `1.0–1.5` → "moderate weekly pattern"; `< 1.0` → "no clear weekly pattern". Skip
      this clause entirely if granularity ≠ day.
    - `noise_ratio`: `< 0.05` → "very stable values"; `0.05–0.15` → "low variability";
      `0.15–0.30` → "noisy"; `> 0.30` → "very noisy".

    Combine into one comma-separated clause. Example for a daily metric:
    *"strong weekly pattern, low variability."*

    **Accept user replies in any reasonable phrasing** — the number, the full friendly name,
    a partial name, or older technical aliases ("MAD", "SDOW", "YoY", "rolling window").
    If the reply is ambiguous, ask once to disambiguate.

    **Eligibility rules — only show methods that are eligible.** Eligibility is evaluated
    against the **resolved timeframe** — `n_points` is the count of non-null buckets the
    timeframe returns.

    | Option | Eligible when |
    |---|---|
    | 1. Let me pick | Always |
    | 2. All time (`mad`) | `n_points ≥ 10` |
    | 3. Weekly basis (`sdow`) | `granularity == day` AND timeframe contains ≥ 8 prior same-weekday observations |
    | 4. Yearly basis (`yoy`) | `granularity == month` AND `n_points ≥ 24` |
    | 5. Rolling basis (`rolling`) | `n_points ≥ 12` |

    If the user picks a method that the timeframe doesn't cover (e.g., picks **Yearly
    basis** with a 30-day timeframe), tell them the gap and offer to either extend the
    timeframe to satisfy the method or pick a different method.

    **If the user picks option 1 (let me pick)**, apply this decision tree internally
    (first match wins) and announce the choice back:

    | # | Condition | Method (internal id) |
    |---|---|---|
    | 1 | `10 ≤ n_points < 30` | All time (`mad`) |
    | 2 | daily granularity AND `weekday_pattern_score > 1.5` | Weekly basis (`sdow`) |
    | 3 | monthly granularity AND `n_points ≥ 24` | Yearly basis (`yoy`) |
    | 4 | otherwise | Rolling basis (`rolling`) |

    Announce with the **user-facing name** and a plain-language reason, e.g.,
    *"Using **Weekly basis** because the metric has a strong weekly pattern."* — never
    *"Picking sdow because weekday_pattern_score is 2.1."*

    ### Step 4 — Compute the classified series in ONE AQL query

    The goal is a per-bucket expectation **and** classification, so the chart in the Output
    Format can mark anomalies directly from the AQL result and the prose can read the
    investigated bucket's status directly from the same record. No re-classification in
    narrative reasoning later.

    Generate ONE AQL query that returns, for each bucket in the look-back window **and** the
    `time_point` bucket, these columns:

    | Column | Description |
    |---|---|
    | `bucket` | The time bucket (date / week / month / quarter), ascending |
    | `actual` | The metric value at that bucket, with user's filters applied |
    | `expected` | The baseline-method's expected value, computed from **preceding** buckets only |
    | `spread` | The baseline-method's σ-equivalent, computed from the same preceding buckets |
    | `expected_lower` | `expected − 2 × spread` |
    | `expected_upper` | `expected + 2 × spread` |
    | `z` | `abs(actual − expected) / spread` (null when `spread` is null) |
    | `direction` | `'above'` when `actual > expected`, `'below'` when `actual < expected` |
    | `anomaly_label` | `'Anomaly'` when `expected` and `spread` are non-null AND `z ≥ 2`; `'Normal'` when both are non-null and `z < 2`; otherwise `null` (insufficient preceding history) |

    Apply the method's formula using AQL window functions over the bucket ordering. Row
    labels here are the **internal id** from the Step 3 naming table; bind to whichever id
    is recorded in the classified-series record's `method` field.

    | Method (internal id → user-facing name) | `expected` per bucket | `spread` per bucket |
    |---|---|---|
    | `mad` (**All time**) | `median(history)` — constant across all rows | `1.4826 × MAD(history)` — constant |
    | `sdow` (**Weekly basis**) | `mean(actual) OVER (PARTITION BY weekday ORDER BY bucket ROWS BETWEEN 8 PRECEDING AND 1 PRECEDING)` | `stdev(actual) OVER (...same partition/window)` |
    | `yoy` (**Yearly basis**) | `LAG(actual, 12) OVER (ORDER BY bucket) × growth_factor`, where `growth_factor = mean(last 12 months) / mean(prior 12 months)` (constant) | `stdev` of the last 12 months — constant |
    | `rolling` (**Rolling basis**) | `mean(actual) OVER (ORDER BY bucket ROWS BETWEEN 12 PRECEDING AND 1 PRECEDING)` | `stdev(actual) OVER (...same window)` |

    Compute `expected_lower`, `expected_upper`, `z`, `direction`, and `anomaly_label` as
    derived expressions in the same query (e.g., a `CASE` over `z` for `anomaly_label`).

    Call `generate_aql` to compose this query. Run `validate_aql` on the result — this query
    is the riskiest in the skill because of the window functions, partitions, and computed
    columns. If validation fails, regenerate once with refinement context; if it still fails,
    invoke the AQL-fallback escape hatch below. Once validation passes, call `execute_aql`
    with `title="<metric> baseline check (<resolved timeframe>)"` to run the query. Call the
    result the **classified series**.

    Like the history series in Step 1, the classified series is **internal state**, not
    user-facing output. Do not render it as a table or any intermediate chart. It feeds the
    final chart in the Output Format section, which is the user's first and only viz from
    this skill.

    For early rows where there aren't enough preceding buckets (e.g., the first 12 rows under
    rolling window), `expected`, `spread`, and dependent columns will be `null`. That's
    expected — those rows simply produce no marker on the chart.

    **AQL-fallback escape hatch.** If AQL window functions are insufficient for the chosen
    method in this workspace, compute `expected`, `spread`, `expected_lower`, `expected_upper`,
    `z`, `direction`, and `anomaly_label` arithmetically in narrative reasoning over the
    history series from Step 1 instead, and surface a brief notice to the user that fallback
    was used. Output structure (chart + prose) is unchanged.

    ### Step 5 — Emit the classified-series record

    Internal-state record (do **not** show to the user yet):

    ```
    {
      method:            "<mad | sdow | yoy | rolling>",   // internal id from Step 3
      n_used:            <int — number of points used by the method>,
      classified_series: [
        { bucket, actual, expected, spread, expected_lower, expected_upper,
          z, direction, anomaly_label }
      ],
      time_point_row:    <the entry whose bucket == time_point>
    }
    ```

    `classified_series` covers the look-back window AND the `time_point` row. The last row
    is the `time_point` bucket. The `2 × spread` threshold corresponds to a fixed 2σ
    sensitivity. Do not expose sensitivity tuning to the user in this version.

    ## Confirm Anomaly

    Classification was already produced in the AQL of Establish Expectation Step 4. This
    stage only reads the result and handles missing-data edge cases.

    ### Step 1 — Read the `time_point` row

    The headline finding is `time_point_row.anomaly_label` from the classified-series record.

    Stop conditions:

    - If `time_point_row.actual` is `null` (no data at that bucket): stop and report
      *"No data found for `<metric>` on `<date>` with filters `<filters>`. Is the date in
      the future, or do filters exclude all data?"*
    - If `time_point_row.anomaly_label` is `null` (insufficient preceding history at the
      `time_point` bucket itself to classify it): stop and report *"Couldn't classify
      `<date>` — insufficient preceding history within the look-back window. Try a longer
      look-back or a different time point."*

    Otherwise, `time_point_row.anomaly_label` is `'Normal'` or `'Anomaly'`, and the
    Output Format section uses it to shape the summary prose.

    The classification is **binary**. There is no severity tier, no multiple-comparisons
    correction. The user's hypothesis is specifically about `time_point`.

    ## Output Format

    Two parts: **chart**, then **summary prose**. Both always appear. **No table.** Only the
    prose text adjusts to `time_point_row.anomaly_label`.

    ### 1. Chart (always — first in the response)

    Call `generate_viz` to compose a multi-line chart over the `bucket`s in
    `classified_series` (look-back window + `time_point`), with **four lines** plotted on
    the same axis:

    - `actual` — primary line, solid and prominent (the metric trajectory)
    - `expected` — secondary line through the baseline's central tendency, dashed or
      lighter weight so it reads as context, not as a competing signal
    - `expected_upper` — upper boundary of the expected range, dashed/light, same muted
      color as `expected_lower`
    - `expected_lower` — lower boundary of the expected range, dashed/light, paired with
      `expected_upper` so the two together visually delimit the tolerance band

    Plus markers (e.g., red dots) on every row where `anomaly_label = 'Anomaly'`, rendered
    so they remain visible against the lines. No special emphasis on the `time_point` row
    beyond its anomaly marker (if any).

    Rows where `expected`, `expected_lower`, or `expected_upper` are `null` (early buckets
    with insufficient preceding history) produce gaps in those three lines — that's correct
    behavior; do not interpolate across them. The `actual` line stays continuous.

    Note: Holistics charts do not render shaded confidence bands — use paired upper/lower
    lines as above, not a filled area.

    Then call `execute_viz` with `title="Anomaly check: <metric> on <resolved time_point>"`
    to render it as a fresh ad-hoc viz inline in the chat. See the top-of-skill **Tool rule
    — never replace the user's canvas**: `execute_viz_block` is forbidden in this skill, no
    exceptions. If the user is currently viewing a chart on the canvas (any tile,
    exploration, or saved view), the investigation's chart must appear as a new chat
    artifact alongside it — never in its place.

    ### 2. Summary prose (always — immediately after the chart)

    Read directly from `time_point_row`. Do not recompute. Two templates only:

    Use the **user-facing name** of the baseline method (from the Step 3 naming table) —
    never the internal id. Translate the raw `z` value into a "Nx the typical fluctuation"
    phrase so the user doesn't have to interpret a z-score.

    - **Normal** (`anomaly_label = 'Normal'`) — *"**<Metric label> on <resolved date> =
      <actual>** sits within the expected range of <expected_lower>–<expected_upper>, so
      it's not anomalous. (Baseline: **<user-facing method name>**.)"*

    - **Anomaly** (`anomaly_label = 'Anomaly'`) — *"**<Metric label> on <resolved date> =
      <actual>** is anomalous: <|actual − expected|> <direction> the expected range of
      <expected_lower>–<expected_upper> — about **<z>× the typical <period> fluctuation**
      (where `<period>` is "day-to-day", "week-to-week", "month-to-month", or
      "quarter-to-quarter" depending on the bound granularity). (Baseline:
      **<user-facing method name>**.) To find the driver, break `<metric>` down by relevant
      dimensions (region, plan, channel, product…) for `<resolved date>` and compare each
      segment's actual vs. its historical share."*

    **If `classified_series` contains OTHER rows with `anomaly_label = 'Anomaly'`** (any
    historical row besides `time_point`), append one sentence: *"Note: <N> other anomalies
    detected in the look-back window — see the red markers on the chart."*

    ### Numeric formatting

    - Currency-like measures (anything whose label or measure name contains `revenue`,
      `cost`, `price`, `arr`, `mrr`, `gmv`, `amount`): format with `$` prefix and short
      suffix (`$500K`, `$1.2M`). Use the dataset's stated currency symbol if metadata
      provides it.
    - Counts and rate measures: format with thousands separators (`1,234`) and two
      significant decimals for rates (`3.42%`).
    - z-scores: one decimal place (`11.4`).

    ## Edge Cases & Graceful Degradation

    Apply these rules at the stage where they apply. If multiple rules match, the earlier
    rule wins.

    | Situation | Behavior |
    |---|---|
    | `list_datasets()` returns an error (e.g., "not supported in development mode") | Ask the user to name the dataset explicitly. Do not proceed. |
    | Metric name doesn't match any measure in the resolved dataset | Show the top-3 closest measure names with descriptions; ask the user. |
    | Time point parses ambiguously (e.g., "last Tuesday" near a weekend boundary, timezone shift) | Echo the resolved absolute date back to the user before any AQL call. |
    | `n_points < 10` after pulling history | Stop. Report "Not enough history (n=X). Need ≥10 comparable points." |
    | The `time_point` row's `actual` is null in `classified_series` (no data at that bucket) | Stop. Report "No data found for `<metric>` on `<date>` with filters `<filters>`. Is the date in the future, or do filters exclude all data?" |
    | The `time_point` row's `anomaly_label` is null (insufficient preceding history at the `time_point` bucket itself) | Stop. Report "Couldn't classify `<date>` — insufficient preceding history within the look-back window. Try a longer look-back or a different time point." |
    | User filter context narrows the history series to `< 10` points | Treat as the `n_points < 10` case. |
    | User picks a method that turns out to be uncomputable (e.g., too few same-weekday observations) | Tell the user and offer to fall back to the next-most-eligible method. |
    | AQL window functions are insufficient for the chosen method in this workspace | Fall back to narrative computation over the Step 1 history series for `expected`, `spread`, `expected_lower`, `expected_upper`, `z`, `direction`, `anomaly_label`. Surface a brief notice that fallback was used. Output structure unchanged. |
    | `execute_aql` errors mid-investigation | Stop. Report which stage failed and surface the AQL error message. Do not pretend the investigation completed. |
    | User asks "why did this happen?" or "what dimension drove it?" | Reply: "I can confirm whether the value is statistically anomalous, but identifying which dimension drove it — or what business event caused it — is outside this skill. Break the metric down by relevant dimensions in a dashboard, or check your marketing calendar / CRM / news for that window." |
    | User asks to "update the chart", "refresh the tile", or otherwise points at an existing chart on the canvas | Do not rerender their tile. Emit the investigation's chart as a fresh ad-hoc viz via `execute_viz` (never `execute_viz_block`). Tell the user the new chart is alongside their existing one, not a replacement. |
    | User asks to "show the raw numbers", "give me the data", or otherwise requests a table of the history or classified series | Do not dump those internal series wholesale. If a table is genuinely useful, render it inline — either via `execute_viz` with a `DataTable { ... }` AML viz (preferred, native Holistics inline table) or as a plain markdown table in the chat reply. Both keep the artifact in chat. Never use `execute_viz_block` or any other dashboard-targeting tool, and never replace the user's current chart with a table. |

    ## Closing reminder

    This skill confirms or denies that a value is statistically anomalous given its history.
    It does NOT identify which dimension drove the anomaly, nor speculate on causes. End the
    response by inviting the user to investigate further with dimensional breakdowns and
